{"ts": 1789783700.251106, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long features 0, 10, and 20 (equal weight, all positive signs). In-sample Sharpe **1.218**.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** mean 1.00, sd 0.30.\n\n### How I got there\n\n1. **Marginal screen** of all 40 features individually. Features 0 (0.80) and 10 (0.78) stood far above the pack; 20 (0.53) and 7 (0.47) were next, then a cluster of weak candidates in the 0.2\u20130.45 range (both signs).\n\n2. **Pair search** among the top ~11 by absolute Sharpe. {0, 10} gave 1.109 \u2014 a large jump over either leg alone, indicating the two carry largely independent information rather than a shared factor.\n\n3. **Triple search** adding each remaining candidate to {0, 10}. Feature 20 was best at 1.218.\n\n4. **Sign verification** on the winner: flipping any leg degraded performance sharply (0.31\u20130.60), confirming all three belong long. I also checked competing triples that drop feature 0 or 10 \u2014 all came in near 1.03, so the chosen combination is the genuine leader, not a coin flip.\n\n### Why I predict below the in-sample number\n\nWith n=5000 the sampling SE on a Sharpe is about 0.22 annualized, so features 0 and 10 at ~3.5\u03c3 are almost certainly real signal \u2014 that part of the result should hold up.\n\nThe third leg is where I discounted. Feature 20's *incremental* gain (+0.11 over the pair) isn't cleanly separable from the +0.05 to +0.09 that several other candidates offered; picking the maximum of that set capitalizes on noise. Most of my 0.2 shrinkage is charged to that choice, with a little more for having selected the best of ~15 pairs.\n\nThe sd of 0.30 covers OOS sampling noise plus the residual possibility that some of this edge is regime-specific rather than structural. One caveat worth flagging: my confidence in features 0 and 10 rests on the assumption that the in-sample period is reasonably representative \u2014 if the OOS window is a different regime, the whole spec could underperform my interval, and that risk is harder to quantify from in-sample data alone."}
